## Git store

A git object store that keeps repositories in S3 and reads them by byte range, written to be lifted into a project of its own. It is the foundation layer of the plan in https://github.com/blotcms/blot/issues/2259: this directory holds the pieces the rest builds on. **Blot does not use it yet.**

Nothing in this directory may require anything outside it except Node built-ins and declared npm packages (`tests/standalone.js` checks). There is no `config`, no `models`, no Redis. Whatever is specific to a host (an S3 client, the bucket and prefix, the cache directory and size, the per-repository lock) is passed in.

## What is here

A repository is a set of immutable packs, each with its `.idx`, and a small manifest for each push. The packs and the manifests sit in an object store, keyed under a prefix the host chooses (`git/{blogID}/`):

```
manifests/<seq>.json     (seq zero-padded to 10 digits, so keys sort in order)
manifests/latest.json    (a hint at the newest seq, nothing more)
packs/pack-<40 hex>.pack
packs/pack-<40 hex>.idx
packs/pack-<40 hex>.rev  (written by receive)
```

- **`objectStore/memory.js`, `objectStore/s3.js`**: the object store interface (`head`, `read`, `get`, `stream`, `put`, `putIfAbsent`, `delete`, `list`, `id`; the contract is written at the top of `memory.js`). The S3 store uses only what Backblaze B2 also supports: plain and multipart PUT, ranged GET, HEAD, DELETE and LIST, plus one conditional write: `putIfAbsent(key, buffer)` resolves true if it created the key and false if the key already existed, and of several racing writers exactly one gets true. On S3 it is a PUT with `If-None-Match: *`; a 412 is false, a 409 (concurrent writers) is retried a few times and then thrown. Nothing else is conditional. A stream of any size is uploaded in parts, read only when an upload slot is free, so memory is about `partSize * concurrency`. The memory store is for specs and counts what it is asked.

- **`ranges.js`**: `readRanges(store, key, ranges)` merges ranges that are close together into one read and runs the reads in parallel with a limit.

- **`cache.js`**: one LRU cache on local disk across all repositories, capped by bytes. Nothing is pinned and nothing is required to be in it: an entry can vanish at any moment, `put` may decline to store something, and a miss costs round trips, never correctness. A damaged or half-written entry (each file has a CRC and length, and is written to a temporary file then renamed) is a miss. It works at any cap, including one too small to hold anything. It also hands out **scratch reservations**: `reserve(bytes)` takes bytes out of the same budget (evicting cached data), gives a directory to write into, fails with `NoRoomError` when the rest of the budget is already reserved, can be `extend`ed, and is `release`d when the push ends. One cache instance should own its directory.

- **`idx.js`**: finds an object in a pack by reading the version 2 `.idx` by range: the 1 KB fanout, then the slice of sorted object IDs and offsets sharing the first byte, both cached. Cold, that is two or three small reads; warm, none.

- **`pack.js`, `inflate.js`, `delta.js`**: read one pack entry by range (its header, then its zlib stream in growing reads), and apply deltas.

- **`reader.js`**: `createReader({ store, cache, packs })` reads objects by ID from the repository's live packs: whole (`read`) or as a stream of chunks (`stream`, for objects stored whole). Delta chains are resolved across packs (`OFS_DELTA` within a pack, `REF_DELTA` to an object in any pack, which is how packs completed by `index-pack --fix-thin` refer to what a push did not send). An object found in several packs is read from the newest. Concurrent lookups which need the same part of an index share one fetch. An index is checked once against the size in the manifest (a HEAD alongside its first fetch), and so is a pack (alongside its first read). Every object read by ID is hashed and compared with the ID asked for, so a flipped bit, a bad offset, a truncated pack or the wrong base is a `CorruptError`, never wrong bytes.

- **`manifest.js`**: the per-repository document: format version, sequence number, refs, the live pack list, a ref snapshot per push, a pointer to the history pack, and the peeled target of each annotated tag a ref points at. `read`, `parse`, `serialize`, `validate`, `advance` (the manifest after a push) and `swap`. Each push writes a new manifest to `manifests/<seq>.json`, which is never overwritten, and the repository is whatever its newest manifest says. **The conditional create of that object is what makes a swap safe**: `swap` calls `putIfAbsent` for `seq + 1`, and if it is false someone swapped first and `swap` throws `StaleManifestError`, writing nothing. A create which lands but loses its response is not mistaken for that: every manifest `advance` makes carries a random `writer` nonce, and when the create answers false or fails in a way which may have applied it (a network error, a timeout, a 5xx; not a 4xx), `swap` reads the manifest at that seq. Its own nonce means the swap landed and it resolves as a success (so does calling it again with the same `next`); another's means the race was lost; no manifest after an error means it did not land and the error is rethrown; and if the read fails too it throws `OutcomeUnknownError` (code `OUTCOME_UNKNOWN`), which a host must not turn into either a success or a failed push. The AWS SDK itself retries a PutObject that fails with a dropped connection, a timeout or a 5xx (three attempts by default), which is exactly how a retry can find the key already created and get a 412. However long a process stalls, it cannot replace a newer manifest, and no single object holds the only copy of a repository's refs. The host's lock (a function passed in) is **not** what makes it safe, because a lock can expire under a process that has stalled. `swap` still takes it, since it stops two pushes from the same base doing wasted work, and checks under it that the base is still the newest so the loser finds out early. It also checks that every pack the new manifest adds is already uploaded, so packs always go up before the manifest that names them. `manifests/latest.json` (`{ "seq": N }`) is written after a swap, best-effort and overwritten each time; a failure to write it does not fail the swap. It is only a hint. `read` GETs it, HEADs `seq + 1`, `seq + 2` and so on until one is missing, and GETs that manifest; if the hint is missing, corrupt, names a manifest which doesn't exist or is more than a few behind, it LISTs `manifests/` instead, and with no manifests at all it reads as empty with seq 0. A numbered manifest never changes, so its bytes are kept in memory by key (a few per store) and only the question of which number is newest goes to the store: the usual read is a GET of the hint, a HEAD, and a GET of the manifest unless this process wrote or already read it; a swap is two HEADs, the conditional PUT and the hint PUT. Old manifests are not deleted; that belongs with compaction. Every push adds a snapshot of the refs to the manifest, so an untrimmed manifest only grows; trimming snapshots belongs with compaction too. Until then the 64 MiB limit `read` applies is what stops a manifest becoming unreadable: `swap` checks the serialised size first and throws `ManifestTooLargeError` (code `MANIFEST_TOO_LARGE`), writing nothing, because a numbered manifest is immutable and one that `read` refused could never be replaced. The manifest is always read from the object store, never the pack cache, because only the store knows which manifest is newest.

- **`objects.js`, `rev.js`, `packscan.js`**: `objects.js` parses commits, trees and tags (`parseCommit`, `parseTree`, `parseTag`), and throws `CorruptError` for bytes that are not one. `rev.js` builds a pack's `.rev` file from its `.idx`. `packscan.js` walks an incoming pack file front to back and reports which delta bases it names but does not contain.

- **`receive.js`**: receiving a push. See below.

- **`layout.js`, `errors.js`, `limit.js`**: key names, error classes (each with a `code`), and a concurrency limiter.

- **`pktline.js`, `advertise.js`, `clone.js`, `fetch.js`, `history.js`**: the read side of smart HTTP, described under *Clone* and *Fetch* below.

## Clone

`advertise(manifest)` returns the body of `GET info/refs?service=git-upload-pack`, from the manifest alone. `serve({ store, cache, manifest, body, out })` answers `POST git-upload-pack` (the request body decompressed, `out` the HTTP response, `cache` optional), and `clone({ store, manifest, packs, out })` is the part of it which writes the pack, of all the manifest's packs or the `packs` given. The host does authentication, the status line and content types (`CONTENT_TYPE` in each file), gunzips the request, and ends up with a handler of a dozen lines (`tests/helpers/upload-server.js` is one).

**Protocol version 0 only.** A client which asks for version 2 (`Git-Protocol: version=2`) gets a version 0 advertisement and falls back, as it does with Blot's endpoint today. The host must not pass that header on. The advertisement offers `multi_ack_detailed`, `no-done`, `side-band-64k`, `ofs-delta`, `no-progress`, `object-format=sha1`, `symref` and `agent`, and nothing else: no `shallow` or `filter` (git itself refuses `--depth` against such a server, and ignores `--filter` with a warning), no `multi_ack`, no `thin-pack`, no `include-tag` (the `^{}` lines let a client follow tags itself). An annotated tag is followed by its peeled `^{}` line when the manifest has one. A request which asks for shallow or a filter anyway gets an `ERR` packet, which git shows as `remote error`, and no pack. So does a want that is not the tip of a ref (now or after a recent push, because a push can land between a client's two requests) and a client without `ofs-delta`.

**What is sent.** A request with no `have` lines gets every pack in the manifest's list, as one pack, whatever was asked for: fewer refs than there are still gets all of them, which is never too little. A request with `have` lines gets the packs a fetch needs (see *Fetch*), planned and streamed by the same code. The history pack is not sent; it only repeats commits. The pack is a header with the exact count of objects, the entries of every pack copied verbatim, and a SHA-1 trailer computed as it goes.

**Duplicates.** The indexes are merged first (their IDs are sorted, so this reads each index once, a slice at a time) to find objects in more than one pack and to get the count for the header. The newest copy is kept, as in `reader.js`. The older ones are skipped, which changes the entries around them: an `OFS_DELTA` names its base by distance, so one which spans a skipped entry gets a shorter distance, and one whose base was a skipped copy becomes a `REF_DELTA` naming the base by ID. A pack with nothing to skip is copied in chunks, without looking inside. A pack with skipped entries is copied up to the first and walked entry by entry from there, inflating each entry's zlib stream only to find where it ends (`endOf` in `clone.js`; see *Where an entry ends*). A `.rev` file can replace that one function later. Rewriting a header can change its length, so the walk notes each entry which changed the size of what follows, to work out the distance of later deltas.

**Every pack in the manifest must be self-contained.** Keeping one copy of a duplicate across packs can only make a delta cycle if a pack's `REF_DELTA` names a base which lives in another pack. `index-pack --fix-thin` prevents that today, because it appends the bases a thin pack needs. So whatever writes packs (Blot's own commits, compaction) must never store a `REF_DELTA` against another pack, or clones can break. History pieces in `manifest.historyPieces` are not sent either, since their commits are in the packs already. A fetch sends only some of the packs, so the same rule is what keeps that subset self-contained.

**A slow plan.** Merging the indexes can take long on a big repository, and a proxy may give up on a response which says nothing. If planning is still going after 5 seconds (`keepaliveAfter`), a side-band client is sent the NAK, one line of progress on band 2 ("Preparing your repository...", left out for a client which sent `no-progress`) and an empty band 1 packet (`0005\x01`, which git's own upload-pack sends too) every 5 seconds until the plan is done. After that a failure can only be reported on band 3. A plan which finishes sooner writes nothing early, so its failure can still be an HTTP status. A client which left while it waited in the limiter is not planned for.

**Failure.** The trailer is the last thing written, and only after every entry was. Any failure on the way (a read which comes back short, a store error, a corrupt entry, a pack not the size the manifest says or not holding the objects it says) stops the transfer without it, so no client can take what it got for a complete pack. With a side-band the client is first sent an error packet and the response is ended; otherwise `out` is destroyed. If the failure comes before the first byte is written (reading the indexes, say) nothing has been written and the host can answer 500. The bytes copied are not checked, so a read which is the right length but wrong would reach the client; the client's own checks (zlib, and object IDs under `transfer.fsckObjects`) are what catch that.

**A client which goes away** (the response closes before the end) stops the reading: nothing new is read, the reads in flight settle quietly, and `clone` rejects with a `ClientGoneError`, which is not a fault. A client which is slow stops the reading too: nothing is read ahead except the few chunks in flight, because the next write waits for `drain`.

**Memory per clone.** A few chunks of 4 MB for the pack being sent (and up to two small packs read ahead), the slice of IDs the merge holds for each pack (8 MB in all, shared out between the packs, though never less than 64 IDs a pack, which is 1.5 KB), an entry which is walked passing through in pieces of at most a chunk, and 36 bytes for each skipped entry and 16 for each entry whose header changed. It does not depend on the size of the repository or on how big one object is. Budget about 50 MB, plus the sockets, for each running clone.

**Capping clones.** `createLimiter({ max, maxWaiting })` is a counter the host shares between all its clones and passes as `limiter`. `max` run at once, `maxWaiting` queue, and a clone beyond that fails with a `BusyError` before writing anything, for the host to answer with 503.

**Cost.** A clone of one pack is one read per chunk. With several packs there are also two reads per pack for each slice of its index, and the walk of any pack with skipped entries inflates everything from its first skipped entry on (CPU only; memory does not grow). Compaction will reduce the number of packs and so the duplicates.

## Fetch

A client that already has part of the repository sends `have` lines, and gets the packs it needs instead of all of them. `serve` answers the negotiation, and `fetch.js` works out which packs.

**Which packs.** The manifest keeps the refs as they were after every push (a snapshot, with the seq of its push). A client that has every tip of a snapshot has everything that was reachable at that push. What it lacks was not reachable then, and the pack of every later push holds what that push added, because a client builds a push's pack against the refs it was shown and Blot's own commits add only what is new. So the packs with a seq above the snapshot's hold everything the client lacks, and those are sent whole. They can also hold objects the client has; that is accepted. A force push needs no special case, because an older snapshot is still the refs the client may have. The newest snapshot the client provably has is the floor.

This relies on two things that other code must keep true. **A pack's `seq` is the last swap that made its objects needed**, so a pack above the floor is sent and one at or below it holds nothing made reachable later. Two things keep this true. A push whose pack is already listed (a force push that restores history, then pushes it again, sends the same pack) does not keep the old entry: `manifest.advance` moves it to the end of the list with the new seq, so a pack is listed once and the list is ordered by seq. Clone's newest-copy rule is unaffected, since it only prefers later packs and every pack is self-contained. And a push with no pack (just refs) that points at a commit no ref reaches, which may be in any old pack, lists all the packs again with its seq (`reachedAgain` in `receive.js`; it reads the history to tell, and a tip that is reachable moves nothing). That costs the next fetch a clone and is rare. Compaction giving a merged pack the largest seq of the packs it merges keeps the invariant, and anything else that writes a manifest (Blot's own commits) passes the packs it uses to `advance`, which does the moving. **Snapshots are only trimmed from the old end**, which lowers the floor and never makes it wrong. The first would break if a push's pack left out an object the repository already held in an older pack but no ref reached. A client builds its pack against the refs it was shown, so it does not, and neither do Blot's commits.

**How the client's place is found.** Two ways, the first tried first.

- The `have` lines list every tip of a snapshot (a tag's tip is the commit it points at, from `peeled`). That needs only the manifest, no request to the object store. Git sends the tips of its own refs first, and the advertised tips it already has, so a client one push behind is found in its first round.
- Otherwise a tip is an ancestor of a have. The commit graph is the history pieces and the history pack (`history.js`), read whole: a request for each of them however long the history is, and none for each commit. They are kept in the cache. The walk from the haves through that graph is in memory and stops after `maxWalk` commits (500,000). A walk that stops early has seen only ancestors, so what it proves is still true; it just proves less. If the history pieces and pack together are over `maxHistory` (64 MiB), or the commits add up to over `maxExpanded` (256 MiB) once inflated, the walk is not done. The second matters because commit messages compress well: each entry's size is taken from the budget from its header before it is inflated, and a delta's result before it is applied, so a pack that expands too far stops at once, not after it is all in memory.

If neither proves a snapshot, the whole repository is sent, as a clone does. That is the answer for a client with an unrelated history, and for one that lacks a tip which has been in the refs since the start (every snapshot has it, so none can be proven; an old tag it never took, say). The spike's unbounded walk, a request for each commit, is gone: not finding anything costs one read of the history and then a clone.

**Tags.** A snapshot's tag is proven by the commit it points at, which does not prove the client has the tag object, and a client that lacks one and asks for it must get it. So once the floor is chosen, each annotated tag the client asked for (those in `peeled`) is read from the packs that would be sent, following tags of tags, and if one is not there everything is sent instead. A tag pushed after the floor is in a later pack, so the usual case is a few reads. A manifest without `peeled` cannot say which wants are tags, nor which of its snapshot tips are tag objects. Fetch reads the history to tell commits from tags, and a snapshot with an annotated tag in it cannot be proven, so a repository like that is sent whole until a push gives its manifest the field.

**The negotiation** is stateless: each request carries the wants and the haves the client was told are common, and is answered from that alone. The advertisement offers `multi_ack_detailed`, which a stateless client needs, because it is how the server says which haves are common and the client replays them in its next request. It also offers `no-done`: when the server is ready, the pack follows the answer in the same response, so a fetch one push behind is one POST. `serve` answers as upload-pack does (`get_common_commits` in git's `upload-pack.c`): `ACK <oid> common` for each have it knows, `ACK <oid> ready` once a snapshot is proven, `NAK`, and after `done` (or with `no-done`) the last common `ACK`, or `NAK` when there is none. A have the repository does not have is not acknowledged, which is what a client that is ahead sends first. If the first round proves a snapshot from the tips alone, only the haves that are tips are acknowledged; otherwise every have that is a commit of the repository is, so that the haves the client replays are enough to prove the same again. A client that did not ask for `multi_ack_detailed` is only sent `NAK`, so it negotiates until it says done, and its last request has no haves to prove anything from: it is sent everything. A snapshot with no refs is proven by any haves at all, but with no common commit there is nothing to acknowledge and the client has shown nothing, so then the floor is 0, there is no `ready`, and the answer is `NAK` (no `ACK` is ever built from an empty list). Git sends commits as haves, never tags.

**Cost.** The usual fetch is one POST. Finding the floor from the haves costs nothing from the object store. Planning and sending the packs costs what a clone of those packs costs: with one pack there is no index to merge, and with several there are two reads per pack. A walk adds a request for each history piece (none when they are cached). Reading history to negotiate holds the same limiter as a clone, since it can hold tens of megabytes, and it happens before any byte is written, so it sends no keepalive.

**Left out.** Shallow and filtered fetches (refused), `include-tag`, `thin-pack`, and finding which packs a tag needs when it is not in the packs chosen (everything is sent).

## Receiving a push

`receive({ store, cache, lock, commands, pack, limit, sizeHint })` takes the ref updates a client sent and the pack that followed them, and either makes them the repository's new manifest or changes nothing. The steps are listed at the top of `receive.js`. The ones that are easy to get wrong:

- **The pack is scratch, and scratch is budget.** The incoming pack is written under a `cache.reserve` reservation, which grows as the pack arrives and again for what `index-pack` writes. A pack over `limit` is refused, and a cache with no room for it fails the push with `NoRoomError`'s message. The reservation is released, and its directory deleted, in every case before `receive` resolves.
- **A thin pack needs its bases.** `packscan.js` finds the `REF_DELTA` bases the pack does not contain; they are read from the store in a batch and written to scratch as loose objects, which `git index-pack --fix-thin --fsck-objects` reads as an alternate. `--fsck-objects` is git's check that every object is well formed. It is not `--strict`, which would also check that links resolve, against a repository that is not on disk. The links are checked in JS.
- **Connectivity only checks what the push adds.** Stored objects are closed under what they point at, so for each new commit its parents and tree must exist, and for a tree only the entries that differ from the same place in the commit's first parent are looked at (a merge compares with its first parent, a root commit checks every entry). A tree in the pack which no commit led to is checked whole. This relies on every stored object being closed, so the orphan sweep and the rewrite script must keep it true. Every ref update's new tip must exist, even when the push carries no objects.
- **A history piece is appended, not rebuilt.** A push that brings commits also uploads a pack of just those commits, listed in the manifest's `historyPieces`. The history pack is a different thing (`history` in the manifest) and is not touched; compaction merges the pieces into it. A reader of the manifest that does not know about pieces can ignore them.
- **Peeled tags are recorded when a tag is pushed.** `manifest.peeled` maps the object ID of each annotated tag that a ref (or a snapshot) names to the object it finally points at. That is what the advertisement's `^{}` line says, and how git knows to fetch tags along with a branch. `receive` finds it by reading the tag (from the push, or from the repository when the tag is already stored) and following tags of tags to the end. The field is optional when read. `manifest.advance` carries it on, adds the push's entries, and keeps only the tags that some snapshot still names, so trimming snapshots later trims it. A manifest that has refs and no `peeled` was written before the field existed and does not know which of its refs are annotated tags, so `advance` leaves it without the field, and fetch treats it with care (see *Fetch*).
- **Uploads come before the swap, and the swap alone decides.** The pack, its `.idx` and `.rev`, and the history piece are uploaded first (`manifest.swap` also checks they are there). A failed upload leaves the manifest as it was. The manifest is then read again and the commands' old values checked against it, so a push may be built on a manifest that has since moved (another push, one of Blot's commits) as long as the refs it names have not.

`receive` does not throw. It resolves with an `outcome`: `ok`, `refused` (nothing changed and retrying the same push will not help: a ref not allowed, a ref that moved, a bad pack, too large, missing objects), `failed` (nothing changed and retrying may help: no room in the cache, the object store would not take an upload; the `error` is for the host's log), or `unknown`. **`unknown` is a swap that may or may not have happened and could not be read back (`OutcomeUnknownError`). It must be reported to the client as neither a success nor a failed push.** The result also carries the report's `status`, `results` and `messages`, worded as `receivePack.js` words them, so the host's HTTP layer only has to frame them. The `unknown` messages tell the user to run `git fetch` before pushing again.

**Progress is streamed.** `receive` takes an optional `progress(message)` and calls it as each stage starts (checking the pack, fetching the files the push builds on, checking the new commits, saving to Blot, saved), in words for a blog owner. `sideband.js` (`createResponse(res, { capabilities, commands })`) turns that into the HTTP response as it happens: each message goes out on band 2 straight away, then the report on band 1 and the flush. A client that sent `quiet` (git does when stderr is not a terminal, unless `--progress`) gets no progress, one without `side-band-64k` gets only the plain report, and a stage silent for five seconds sends `0005\x01`, the empty packet git's own receive-pack uses to keep connections alive (the spec checks a real git accepts it). Once the first byte is out the HTTP status cannot change, so every ending is a report: `finish(result)` for a result, `fail(err)` for anything else, which reports every ref as failed and tells the user to run `git fetch` before pushing again.

The refusals are `receivePack.js`'s: only master may be a branch, master cannot be deleted, and tags are kept. The host still owes the rest of what `receivePack.js` does around this: parsing the request, authentication, `validateTree` (submodules, symlinks, file modes), the follow-up sync alarm and the after-push callback.

## Blot's own commits

Blot's own writes (a preview file, a template file) are one commit, one pack and one manifest swap each. Grouping several writes into one commit, pack or swap was considered and rejected on 2 Nov 2026. Writes are infrequent and small, setup does not use `client.write` because `create.js` makes its own batches, and the one slow case is a large dashboard upload, which compaction and manifest pruning clean up afterwards. Do not add grouping without new evidence that writes are frequent or large.

## Where an entry ends

The `.idx` gives where an entry starts, not where it ends. There are two ways to find the end: a `.rev` file (the pack's offsets in order), or inflating the entry and seeing where its zlib stream stops. This store inflates.

- Reading an object means inflating its entry anyway, so the end costs nothing extra. A `.rev` lookup would be a binary search of extra ranged reads per object, since it maps pack order to index order and not the other way round.
- Inflating checks the entry as it goes: zlib verifies the stream's checksum, and the output must match the size in the entry header exactly. That is how truncated and damaged data is caught.
- It needs nothing but the `.pack` and `.idx`. A `.rev` is only written by newer git, or by an option of `index-pack`, and Blot's own commits would have to write one too.

The cost is that finding an entry's end without wanting its data (to copy its compressed bytes verbatim into a clone, say) means inflating it and discarding the output. A pass over a whole pack for that is CPU, not memory or round trips. For that case receive writes a `.rev` beside each pack it uploads, so streaming a clone can use it; nothing in the reader does.

## Memory

Reads are bounded. Ranged reads are at most the caller's range (merged reads are capped at 8 MB). An entry is inflated in pieces and the input is only fetched as zlib asks for it. Objects stored whole can be streamed in chunks of about 16 KB. An object that is a delta is resolved in memory, so `maxSize` (default 64 MB, per call or per reader) caps the base, the deltas and the result, and a bigger object is a `TooLargeError`.

## Specs

`tests/fetch.js` pushes with a real `git push` through `receive` and fetches and pulls with a real `git` after one push, several, a force push and tag pushes, for clients that are ahead, unrelated, or lack a tip, ending in `git fsck --strict` and refs equal to the server's. It counts requests for a fetch one push behind, cold and warm, and asks the negotiation questions directly. `tests/history.js` reads history packs made by git, deltas included, and `tests/peeled.js` covers peeled tags in the manifest and the advertisement. `tests/clone.js` and `tests/advertise.js` serve clones to a real `git` client over a small HTTP server (`tests/helpers/upload-server.js`), with `transfer.fsckObjects` and then `git fsck --strict`, and count the requests made. The other specs here build repositories with real `git` (delta chains, `OFS_DELTA` and `REF_DELTA` packs, a thin pack completed with `index-pack --fix-thin`, objects in several packs, a tag) and compare every object read through the store with `git cat-file`. `tests/helpers/faults.js` wraps any object store to fail on a schedule (an error after the write landed or before it, a truncated body, a delay, a stale answer), and `tests/faults.js` uses it for ambiguous swaps, cut-short reads, a push which dies between its uploads and a stale hint. `tests/receive.js` pushes with a real `git push` (through the small smart-HTTP server in `tests/helpers/receiveServer.js`) and calls `receive` directly with packs built by git, against the memory store and the fault-injecting one; it asserts the request counts of a one-file push. `tests/s3.js` runs the S3 store against the MinIO the test runner starts (`.github/actions/start-minio`); without `BLOT_STORAGE_ENDPOINT` it is skipped and says so. The cache specs run at caps of a few hundred bytes.

## Not here yet

Materializing a folder, Blot's own commits, compaction (and so the history pack itself, and `.rev` files for packs compaction writes), and the rewrite script. They build on the pieces above. Cache keys include the object store's `id`, so one cache can serve many repositories.
